topics.theoryContent:
Two special forms collect an unknown number of arguments:

*args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple.
**kwargs collects extra keyword arguments into a dictionary.

def total(*args):
    return sum(args)

print(total(1, 2, 3))          # 6
print(total(10, 20))           # 30

def show(**kwargs):
    for key, value in kwargs.items():
        print(key, value)

show(name="Ravi", role="Admin")    # name Ravi / role Admin

These are used when a function genuinely does not know how many arguments it will receive — like a function that sums any list of numbers, or a wrapper that passes options through to another function.

The names args and kwargs are convention, not syntax — but every Python developer uses those exact names, so follow the convention. You will rarely need them in early projects; learn to recognise them when you meet them in library code.

add() inserts one element; discard() and remove() delete one.

attendees = set()
attendees.add("Ravi")       # {"Ravi"}
attendees.add("Anita")      # {"Ravi", "Anita"}
attendees.add("Ravi")       # no change — already present
attendees.discard("Anita")  # {"Ravi"}
attendees.remove("Ravi")    # {}

The difference between remove and discard matters on missing items:
- remove(x) raises a KeyError if x is absent.
- discard(x) silently does nothing if x is absent.

Use discard when you just want "make sure it is gone"; use remove when an absent value is a genuine error.

