topics.theoryContent:
When a child needs its own __init__, it usually wants to set up the parent's attributes too. Call the parent's __init__ with super():

class Employee:
    def __init__(self, name, salary):
        self.name = name
        self.salary = salary

class Manager(Employee):
    def __init__(self, name, salary, team_size):
        super().__init__(name, salary)     # runs Employee's __init__
        self.team_size = team_size

m = Manager("Anita", 90000, 12)
print(m.name, m.salary, m.team_size)

super().__init__(...) calls the parent's __init__, passing the parent's arguments. The child then adds its own attributes. Without it, the parent's attributes (name, salary) would never be set.

The rule: if the child's __init__ needs the parent's setup, call super().__init__(...) FIRST, then set child-specific attributes.

