topics.theoryContent:
A child can REPLACE an inherited method by defining a method with the same name. The child's version wins for child objects.

class Employee:
    def pay(self):
        return self.salary

class Manager(Employee):
    def pay(self):
        return self.salary + 20000      # manager bonus

The override keeps the same method name — callers use pay() for any employee and get the right behaviour without knowing the type. This is POLYMORPHISM: the same call, different behaviour per type. More on that shortly.

A method can also EXTEND the parent's version by calling it inside the override:

def pay(self):
    base = super().pay()
    return base + 20000

