topics.theoryContent:
Polymorphism means "many forms": the same method call behaves differently depending on the object's actual type.

class Employee:
    def pay(self):
        return self.salary

class Manager(Employee):
    def pay(self):
        return self.salary + 20000

staff = [Employee("Ravi", 50000), Manager("Anita", 70000), Manager("Sita", 65000)]
for person in staff:
    print(person.name, person.pay())

The loop calls pay() on every person — but Employees get the plain salary and Managers get the bonus. The loop never checks types; each object knows its own behaviour. That is polymorphism in action.

The payoff: code written against the PARENT type works for every child automatically. You add a new employee type, override pay(), and existing code handles it with zero changes.

codeExamples.code:
class Employee:
