topics.theoryContent:
The property decorator turns a method into an attribute that runs a check on every access or assignment:

class BankAccount:
    def __init__(self, balance):
        self._balance = balance

    @property
    def balance(self):
        return self._balance

    @balance.setter
    def balance(self, value):
        if value < 0:
            raise ValueError("Balance cannot be negative")
        self._balance = value

a = BankAccount(1000)
print(a.balance)       # reads via the getter — no brackets
a.balance = 2000       # writes via the setter — the rule runs
# a.balance = -5  → raises ValueError

Outside code still writes account.balance = ..., but the SETTER intercepts it and enforces the rule. You get attribute-style syntax with method-style control. This is encapsulation at its most elegant.

