topics.theoryContent:
Bubble sort repeatedly steps through the list, swapping adjacent items that are in the wrong order. After each full pass, the largest remaining value "bubbles" to the end.

def bubble_sort(nums):
    n = len(nums)
    for i in range(n - 1):
        for j in range(n - 1 - i):
            if nums[j] > nums[j + 1]:
                nums[j], nums[j + 1] = nums[j + 1], nums[j]
    return nums

Trace [3, 1, 2]: pass 1 compares 3>1 (swap) then 3>2 (swap) → [1, 2, 3]. The inner range shrinks by i because the last i items are already placed.

Simple to understand, but slow: it does n² comparisons, so 10,000 items means ~100 million steps. Its virtue is simplicity — that is why it is taught first.

