Selection sort finds the SMALLEST remaining value on each pass and swaps it into its correct position:

def selection_sort(nums):
    n = len(nums)
    for i in range(n):
        smallest = i
        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            if nums[j] < nums[smallest]:
                smallest = j
        nums[i], nums[smallest] = nums[smallest], nums[i]
    return nums

Trace [4, 2, 9, 1]: the smallest (1) goes to position 0, then 2 to position 1, and so on. After i passes, the first i positions are sorted and never touched again.

Like bubble sort, it is O(n²) — one full scan per position. But it does far fewer swaps: at most n swaps total, versus n² for bubble. That matters when swapping is expensive.

